# msg-gway client

When this rule is invoked, wire **this** service to msg-gway: hand over **session files**, drive I/O over HTTP, consume inbound from Kafka. Language-agnostic.

This service must **not** run TDLib, WhatsMeow, or a Matrix sync loop. The gateway does. Do not put `TELEGRAM_API_ID` / `HASH` here. One Bearer credential per calling service.

## Surface

```text
HTTPS  https://messaging.nottinfra.co.uk
Auth   Authorization: Bearer {credential-id}.{secret}
Topic  msg-gway.{credential-id}
```

`MSG_GWAY_URL`, `MSG_GWAY_TOKEN`, `KAFKA_URL` (from `settings.cfg` `ENDPOINTS.KAFKA` via `NETWORK`). Optional `KAFKA_PRIVATE_KEY` if sessions use `public_key`.

Mint the token in the **msg-gway** repo (`./cmd/service/create-credential.ps1 {this-project}`), store it in **this** service’s Vault.

Commands: JSON in / JSON out. `{ "ok": false }` on 200 is a failed command (no error object). 401/429 also `{ "ok": false }`. Media GET is **raw bytes**.

W3C trace context: send `traceparent` (optional `tracestate`, `baggage`) on command HTTP. The gateway continues that trace. Kafka records carry the same fields as **record headers**, never in the Event/envelope JSON. `command_url` POSTs from the gateway include those HTTP headers. Extract on consume; ignore if missing. Telegram/WhatsApp inbound starts a **new** trace at the gateway, then injects it on Kafka.

Layout in this service:

```text
src/utils/msg-gway        # HTTP client + envelope Open
src/bg-services/msg-gway  # Kafka consumer
src/use-cases/msg-gway    # HandleEvent + outbound helpers
```

---

## Session files (`POST /v1/sessions` → `files`)

The integration interface **is** the file map. Keys are **relative paths** (no `..`, no absolute). Values are **standard Base64** of the file bytes (not base64url). The gateway writes them under its session dir and starts the driver. The row is **not stored** until Start reports authorized → otherwise `{ "ok": false }`.

```json
{
  "platform": "telegram | whatsapp | matrix | custom",
  "files": { "relative/path": "<standard-base64>" },
  "public_key": "",
  "listen": { "chats": ["*"], "usernames": [], "secret_chats": true }
}
```

Success: `{ "ok": true, "session_id": "uuid", "platform": "…", "listen": {…}, "dir": "…" }`. Persist `session_id` in **this** service (DB / config). Reuse it after **this** container restarts — do **not** `POST /v1/sessions` again for the same store.

The gateway keeps the session row and files across **its** restarts and re-attaches the driver (`Restore`). Incoming continues onto the same Kafka topic; outbound still uses the same `session_id`. Only `DELETE /v1/sessions` tears it down. Re-uploading the same Telegram/WhatsApp store while it is already live logs the device out.

`listen` omitted → all chats (DMs **and** groups). `public_key` (RSA PEM) → Kafka values sealed (see Envelope). Omit for plaintext.

`listen.secret_chats: true` → Kafka **only** events with `secret: true` (Telegram secret chats). `false` drops secret events. Omit the field → both. WhatsApp/Matrix events are never `secret`, so `true` silences them.

**Never** start two processes against the same Telegram/WhatsApp store (`AUTH_KEY_DUPLICATED` / device logged out). Treat `files` as secrets (do not log). Copy stores only while the source client is **disconnected**.

### `telegram` — official TDLib directory

Gateway uses `tdl` + `prebuilt-tdlib`. If `tdlib-db/` is present it is `databaseDirectory`; otherwise the session root. If `tdlib-files/` is present it is `filesDirectory`; otherwise `files/`.

Start is authorized when **any** of these names exist **anywhere** in the uploaded tree: `td.binlog`, `db.sqlite`, `db.sqlite-wal`. An empty / QR-pending / logged-out store → `{ok:false}`.

Upload the **whole** logged-in TDLib dir, including WAL, so SQLite is consistent:

```json
"files": {
  "tdlib-db/td.binlog": "<b64>",
  "tdlib-db/db.sqlite": "<b64>",
  "tdlib-db/db.sqlite-wal": "<b64>",
  "tdlib-db/db.sqlite-shm": "<b64>"
}
```

Also valid: `td.binlog` / `db.sqlite` at the map root (no `tdlib-db/` prefix). Optional media cache: `tdlib-files/…` or `files/…`. Do not rely on cache files for auth.

`chat_id` is the Telegram chat id **as a decimal string** (user, basic group, or `-100…` supergroup). Groups the account is in are published the same as DMs unless `listen.chats` / `secret_chats` filters them. `params.username` (`@user` or `user`) or `params.secret_chat: true` + `params.user_id` (Telegram user id) for secret chats. Outbound media: `params.path` if the file is already on the gateway session disk, or **`params.data`** (standard base64 of the bytes) plus `params.filename` when the caller cannot see that disk — Telegram writes it under `files/` then sends.

**Secret chat soak (Telegram only):** send (or create) may return `{ok:true}` while the handshake is still pending. The gateway keeps **at most one** pending secret chat per peer, queues outbound payloads in Postgres + session PVC (`outbound/{id}/`), and sends when TDLib reaches Ready. Kafka `op=chat` for secret chats is emitted **only on Ready** (not pending). `secretChatStateClosed` / send errors fail the queue **immediately**. Only “still waiting for accept” uses TTL (default **60 days**, `OUTBOUND_QUEUE_TTL`). **No** auto-recreate; call send again if the product still wants to talk. WhatsApp has no equivalent.

### `whatsapp` — WhatsMeow SQLite

Gateway opens `sqlstore` on the first matching file, then `GetFirstDevice`. Authorized only if `client.Store.ID != nil` (paired device). Empty QR database → `{ok:false}`.

Preferred key: **`whatsmeow.db`** at the map root. Also accepted, in order: `session.db`, `store.db`, `whatsapp.db`, else the first `*.db` in the tree.

```json
"files": {
  "whatsmeow.db": "<b64>",
  "whatsmeow.db-wal": "<b64>",
  "whatsmeow.db-shm": "<b64>"
}
```

Include `-wal` / `-shm` siblings when they exist. After upload, do not keep another WhatsMeow client open on that same sqlite.

`chat_id` is a JID: `447xxxxxxxxx@s.whatsapp.net` or `…@lid` (DM), **`…@g.us`** (group). Groups the paired device is in are listened and can be sent to with that JID. `params.phone_number` (digits) only for DMs when you do not have a JID. WhatsApp outbound media still needs **`params.path`** on the gateway disk (`params.data` is Telegram-only). `delete_for_everyone` on unsend. `DELETE /v1/chats` = app-state delete + archive.

### `matrix`

JSON with `homeserver`, `access_token`, and `user_id`. Optional `device_id`. Filename **`session.json`** or **`matrix.json`** (any `.json` in the dir that has those fields also works). Start calls `GET /_matrix/client/v3/account/whoami`.

```json
"files": {
  "session.json": "<b64 of {\"homeserver\":\"https://matrix.example\",\"user_id\":\"@u:example\",\"access_token\":\"syt_…\",\"device_id\":\"…\"}>"
}
```

`chat_id` is a room id (`!…:server`).

### `custom`

Optional JSON `{ "command_url": "https://this-service/internal/msg-gway" }` as `session.json` / `custom.json`. Start **always** succeeds. If `command_url` is set, the gateway POSTs `{ok}` JSON to `{command_url}/{send|edit|read|delete_message|create_chat|delete_chat}` with the same fields as the HTTP commands. If omitted, the session is **ingest-only** (`POST /v1/ingest` from this service).

